<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  QBX-276: AFTER A REDO, AN UNDO RUNS THE INVERSE OF THE REDO, not the inverse
  the action produced the first time.

  midi_rec_union_r2 SEGFAULTed intermittently after its last assertion. Under
  ASan it is a heap-use-after-free on every run, in verify-undo's first undo.
  place-midi-recording holds its plan (the merge-columns child above all)
  because the inverse borrows from it, and it re-plans from scratch on every
  apply: a redo deleted the first plan. SActionUndoCommand kept the FIRST
  inverse, though, and SActionHistory deleted the one the redo returned. So
  the next undo's unmerge-columns read its operands out of a freed
  SMergeColumnsAction. In an ordinary build the allocator usually hands that
  block straight back to the re-plan's own merge, which is why it passed most
  of the time.

  place-recording holds and rebuilds its plan the same way. The fix is in the one place the rule can
  live: a redo hands its inverse back, and the undo command adopts it.

  THIS CASE GATES THE PROTOCOL, NOT ONE VERB. probe-reapply's inverse borrows
  its forward exactly as unmerge-columns borrows its merge, and it knows which
  application stamped it. It answers in integers, so an ordinary build sees a
  stale inverse whatever the allocator does. Three undo/redo rounds: the
  first undo runs the first application's inverse (fine either way); the
  second and third run after a redo, and under the old protocol both are
  stale.
-->
<SActionScript version="1" name="undo_redo_adopts_inverse">

  <setup project="new"/>

  <actions>
    <probe-reapply/>
    <undo/>
    <redo/>
    <undo/>
    <redo/>
    <undo/>
    <redo/>
    <assert-reapply-probe stale="0" undos="3"/>
  </actions>

  <assertions>
    <assert-track-count equals="0"/>
  </assertions>

</SActionScript>
